TI  - Predictive maintenance in semiconductor manufacturing: Comparative analysis of machine learning models for downtime reduction
T2  - COMPUTERS & INDUSTRIAL ENGINEERING
LA  - English
KW  - Predictive Maintenance
KW  - Machine Learning Models
KW  - Artificial Neural Networks (ANN)
KW  - Data Preprocessing
KW  - Industrial Downtime Prediction
AB  - Accurate machine downtime prediction is essential for optimizing industrial operations, yet addressing high variability and complex data patterns remains a critical challenge. This study evaluates the performance of five machine learning models-Artificial Neural Networks (ANN), Support Vector Regression (SVR), Multi-Layer Perceptron (MLP), Random Forest Regressor (RFR), and Autoregressive Integrated Moving Average (ARIMA)-on a real-world dataset from a semiconductor manufacturing facility. This study directly addresses the critical challenge of unplanned machine downtime, a persistent issue in the semiconductor industry known for its high-precision and cost-sensitive operations. By analyzing over 1,000,000 time-stamped records, the research highlights the adaptability of advanced machine learning techniques to reduce production disruptions and enhance operational efficiency. The models were assessed across three distinct data patterns: aggregated daily records, machine-specific data, and randomized structures. Performance was evaluated using Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE), Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE), and Error Median Error (EME). ANN achieved the best results, with an RMSE of 179.25, MAPE of 34.30%, and EME of 34.30% in the most challenging randomized pattern, significantly outperforming traditional models such as ARIMA (RMSE: 334.43, MAPE: 59.40%). MLP ranked second, with an RMSE of 201.99 and MAPE of 38.65%. The study also highlights the limitations of traditional models, including ARIMA and SVR, which struggled to handle noisy and dynamic data. This research contributes to the field by demonstrating the critical role of advanced preprocessing techniques, such as data shuffling and feature engineering, in enhancing model generalization. It also bridges the gap between accuracy and scalability, emphasizing the need for hybrid approaches to address computational efficiency in real-time applications. The findings provide a foundation for deploying neural network models in predictive maintenance systems and underline the potential of integrating explainable AI and IoT technologies to improve industrial resilience. Future research should focus on cross-industry validation and adaptive learning techniques to ensure the long-term effectiveness of predictive maintenance solutions.
