TI  - A review on prognostics and health management applications with federated learning for advanced engineering systems
T2  - RELIABILITY ENGINEERING & SYSTEM SAFETY
LA  - English
KW  - Prognostics and health management
KW  - Federated learning
KW  - Predictive maintenance
KW  - Remaining useful life
KW  - Data privacy
KW  - Industrial AI
AB  - Modern engineering systems are increasingly complex and interconnected, making their health management and operational reliability more challenging. Prognostics and Health Management (PHM) enables condition monitoring, fault diagnosis, and Remaining Useful Life (RUL) prediction, yet conventional centralized approaches face regulatory barriers, privacy risks, and difficulty in modelling heterogeneous sensor data, while incurring high communication costs at the edge. Federated Learning (FL) offers a decentralized and privacy-preserving paradigm for collaborative model training without raw data sharing. This review provides a comprehensive synthesis of PHM technologies, FL methodologies, and their integration for distributed, privacy-sensitive industrial environments. We first survey advances in physics-based, data-driven, and hybrid prognostic methods, together with intelligent maintenance and condition-based decision strategies, critically evaluating their adaptability under heterogeneous, resource-constrained settings. We then classify FL approaches by data distribution, network architecture, and participant type, and assess their applicability to PHM tasks through quantitative comparison of privacy-preserving techniques and lightweight deployment strategies. Building on this, we establish a structured mapping between PHM challenges and FL mechanisms, analyse applications in RUL prediction, fault diagnosis, and health monitoring, and identify unresolved gaps. The review concludes with a forward-looking roadmap covering domain-adaptive FL algorithms, resource-efficient edge architectures, and verifiable privacy mechanisms, as well as integration with emerging technologies such as large language models and digital twins. By bridging methodological advances with industrial deployment realities, this work offers actionable guidance for developing scalable, secure, and high-performance PHM solutions in the Industrial Internet of Things era.
