AB  - The building sector is one of the largest contributors to global energy consumption and CO2 emissions. Moreover, energy demand by buildings is projected to increase by 65% by 2050. Digital Twin (DT) technology represents a promising approach to optimize the energy performance of buildings and their sustainability. In this paper, the applications of DT modeling approaches to buildings and their energy performance are critically reviewed. DT models can be integrated with advanced technologies such as Building Information Modeling (BIM), the Internet of Things (IoT), Artificial Intelligence (AI), and Machine Learning (ML). The reviewed studies cover a wide range of building systems, including Heating, Ventilation, and Air Conditioning (HVAC) optimization, lighting control, predictive maintenance, and building energy management. The findings indicate that deployment of DT modeling in buildings can reduce energy consumption by up to 30%, improve indoor thermal comfort by 20%, and lower maintenance costs by 40%. Integration with AI and ML algorithms further enhances the predictive and adaptive capabilities of DTs. Notably, HVAC fault detection accuracies can exceed 95%, and lighting energy savings can reach up to 79%. Some reported case studies have indicated that coupling building DTs with cloud computing and edge devices improves system resiliency and real-time responsiveness. The review analysis of the five-dimensional DT framework reveals that while most implementations address the physical, virtual, data, and service dimensions, only a few DT applications achieve full bi-directional connectivity, indicating that closedloop automated control remains an open challenge. However, future research is needed for large-scale field validation of DT approaches, development of lifecycle-based frameworks, and integration of DT with hybrid ML modeling techniques to enhance their scalability, interoperability, and economic feasibility.
