TI  - DeFIANT: A degradation-aware large model paradigm for generalizable RUL prediction
T2  - MECHANICAL SYSTEMS AND SIGNAL PROCESSING
LA  - English
KW  - Large Language Model
KW  - LLM
KW  - Large Model
KW  - Prediction
KW  - Remaining Useful Life (RUL)
KW  - PROGNOSTICS
KW  - MACHINERY
AB  - Accurately predicting the remaining useful life (RUL) of bearings over long horizons in cross-domain settings remains a central challenge in predictive maintenance, owing to the compounded difficulties posed by complex degradation dynamics and distributional shifts across diverse operating environments. Conventional deep learning methods often prove inadequate in this context, as they are hindered by insufficient signal representation, limited model capacity, and fragile adaptation mechanisms. The recent emergence of large Foundation Models (FMs) offers unprecedented opportunities to address these challenges; however, directly applying language-pretrained FMs to noisy, high-frequency vibration signals remains non-trivial due to fundamental modality mismatches and severe domain shifts. In this work, we therefore propose a hierarchical FM adaptation framework comprising three complementary modules that systematically converts FMs into degradation-aware predictors. Central to our approach is an adaptive signal-representation module that transforms raw vibration signals into structured degradation tokens, enabling the FM to parse bearing degradation as a learnable "language" and thereby bridge the critical modality gap. Building on this representation, we retrofit the frozen FM backbone with a reversible instance-normalization front-end and a triple-scale embedding head. This architectural adaptation suppresses cross-domain covariate shifts before the model and fuses multi-scale degradation information after it, effectively converting the FM into a degradation-aware long-horizon forecaster. Crucially, our framework is guided by a unified domain generalization theory, we derived a generalization error bound for FM-based cross-domain adaptation and evidence that frozen self-attention acts as a PCA-like compressor-we introduce a progressive, integrity-preserving fine-tuning strategy. This strategy selectively activates and calibrates the pretrained FM's generalization capacity while preserving the core integrity of its attention mechanism, thereby boosting cross-domain prediction accuracy without incurring additional parameter burden. Extensive experiments on benchmark bearing datasets confirm that our framework significantly outperforms state-of-the-art methods by up to 33.5% in cross-domain long-horizon RUL prediction.
