AB  - Intelligent operation and maintenance systems have become an indispensable component of modern buildings. As the core infrastructure for regulating indoor environments, the intelligent monitoring of Heating, Ventilation, and Air Conditioning (HVAC) systems is critical to ensuring both energy efficiency and occupant comfort, constituting a central goal of smart building operation and maintenance systems. Deep learning, particularly neural network-based methods, has introduced transformative capabilities to this field. However, with the rapid emergence of diverse backbone architectures, there remains a lack of systematic evaluation regarding their relative strengths and weaknesses in specific HVAC-related tasks. To address this gap, this study benchmarks eight state-of-the-art backbone neural networks across three representative HVAC datasets, focusing on two key tasks: fault diagnosis (classification) and parameter prediction (regression). Through comprehensive comparative experiments, we elucidate the performance characteristics and application suitability of each model. The findings not only provide practical guidance for model selection in smart building research and deployment but are also extendable to other domains with similar data distribution characteristics.
