AB  - The proactive strengthening technique leveraging the shape memory properties of iron-based shape memory alloy (Fe-SMA) can markedly enhance the durability and strength of buildings. A critical factor affecting the effectiveness of this strengthening method is the bond behavior between Fe-SMA and parent structures. However, due to the multitude of influencing factors, especially the effect of long-term hygrothermal environments, predicting bond strength is complex and time-consuming. In light of these challenges, machine learning (ML) offers an efficient scheme for predicting bond strength. This study uses explainable ML models to predict the bond strength of Fe-SMA/steel single joints (SJs). The performance of Spearman-based, importance-based, and norm-based feature selection methods is investigated to identify the most representative feature combinations. Sparrow Search Algorithm (SSA), Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO), Gorilla Troops Optimizer (GTO), and African Vultures Optimization Algorithm (AVOA) are employed to optimize the hyper-parameters of random forest (RF) and back-propagation neural network (BPNN). Additionally, the best-performing model is interpreted utilizing Shapley Additive Explanation (SHAP). The results demonstrate that GTO and SSA enhance the performance of RF and BPNN to the greatest extent, respectively. Compared to unoptimized algorithms, the prediction accuracy of GTO-RF and SSA-BPNN gets improved by 29.98 % and 44.15 %, respectively. Guaranteeing the quality of adhesives is essential for the mechanical performance of Fe-SMA-bonded strengthening structures, given the strong correlation between adhesive property and bond strength. Feature dependence analysis identifies that the effective length of SJs approaches 125 mm. This study offers valuable and innovative solutions for improving the quality and efficiency of Fe-SMA-bonded strengthening design for building maintenance. Its primary advantage lies in the integration of multiple optimization algorithms and explainable ML techniques. These approaches not only improve prediction accuracy but also enhance model interpretability, providing more reliable guidance for practical engineering applications.
