KW  - SPEED
AB  - The Electric Submersible Pump (ESP) system plays a pivotal role in ensuring efficient oil production, particularly in offshore oilfields. However, frequent failures of Electric Submersible Pump Motors, which account for more than half of all ESP failures, often result in significant economic losses. To address this challenge, this study introduces a novel fault diagnosis framework that integrates MOTOR CURRENT SIGNAL ANALYSIS (MCSA) with advanced machine learning techniques. A graph-based learning architecture is designed by employing Graph Neural Networks (GNN) to simultaneously capture the temporal and structural characteristics of current signals, while ensemble learning is incorporated to enhance robustness and generalization. This methodological design represents a substantial contribution to Artificial Intelligence (AI)-based signal processing and fault classification. Building upon this innovation, the proposed framework is applied to fault diagnosis of ESP motors. Real-world data from the Bohai Oilfield in China are used for validation, and experimental results demonstrate that the method achieves a diagnosis accuracy of 89.9%. These findings demonstrate that the proposed framework enhances ESP system reliability by enabling early fault detection and predictive maintenance. By learning representative fault patterns from motor current signals through MCSA and accurately identifying incipient failures using GNN, the method allows timely maintenance decisions, thereby reducing unplanned shutdowns and extending equipment lifetime.
