AB  - Underwater Wireless Sensor Networks (UWSNs) play a critical role in collecting data for various marine applications, but their deployment and maintenance in harsh underwater environments pose significant challenges. Building upon our prior conceptual work, this article presents a practical implementation of an AI-driven fault detection system for UWSNs, a key component of a future predictive maintenance framework incorporating Named Data Networking (NDN). Using the "Underwater Sensor Dataset," we implemented a feed-forward neural network to classify sensor readings as healthy or faulty. The model achieved high accuracy (99.9%), precision (100%), recall (99.1%), and F1-score (99.6%) on the test set, demonstrating its effectiveness in fault detection. Our findings highlight the feasibility and potential of AI-based predictive maintenance in UWSNs, even with a simpler neural network model. The framework promises reduced downtime, minimized maintenance costs, and an extended lifespan for UWSNs.
