AB  - Shared and open offices have grown in demand and value in recent years. In these offices, no principal agent is available for space management because seats are not designated to specific users, posing difficulties in lighting and temperature control, and facility management. This problem can be resolved by collecting data on the conditions of indoor office spaces using Internet of Things (IoT) devices, and providing the data to facility managers for spatial management. However, user privacy must not be undermined during data collection. Therefore, this study analyzes the feasibility of anomaly analysis using deep learning, based on data collected by IoT devices on the conditions of indoor spaces, for facility management. We fabricated three IoT devices that collect data on temperature, humidity, and brightness, and installed them in the underground communal spaces and rest spaces of an office building. Each device collected 53900 time-series data records in about a month, which were used for training a long-short term memory (LSTM) model, which is a representative recurrent neural network method. When the LSTM was trained using the temperature, humidity, and brightness data collected by one device, the prediction accuracy was significantly high with a mean absolute percentage error (MAPE) of 1.47%. When trained using the values obtained by one device based on the data collected by the other two devices installed nearby, the prediction accuracy was very high with a MAPE of 8.58%. Therefore, anomaly analysis can be performed using the model developed through training by setting thresholds for criteria errors.
