TI  - Remaining useful life prediction of mechanical components using multistate transfer learning and multiphase control charts
T2  - KNOWLEDGE-BASED SYSTEMS
LA  - English
KW  - Prognostics and health management
KW  - Condition monitoring
KW  - Health prediction
KW  - Mechanical system
AB  - Accurate prediction of the remaining useful life (RUL) is crucial for enabling predictive maintenance and ensuring the safe and efficient operation of mechanical equipment. However, existing research works typically employ a single-state prognostics model for RUL prediction, without accounting for stage-specific variations in degradation behaviors, which may not adequately capture the inherently multiphase nature of the degradation process. To this end, we propose a RUL prediction approach using multistate transfer learning and multiphase control charts for mechanical components subject to varying operational conditions. Given the stage-specific variations in component degradation, a multiphase autoregressive (MPAR) model is first presented to characterize degradation behaviors, and a multiphase control chart based on this model is developed to dynamically identify each degradation stage. Building on the transition mechanism of the multiphase control chart, a multistate transfer learning approach using deep correlation alignment is then proposed for RUL prediction under varying operating conditions, in which a multiscale weighted convolutional neural network (MSWCNN) is introduced to learn stage-dependent degradation features and supply transferable representations for crosscondition adaptation. Finally, a prognosis framework driven by the transition mechanism of the multiphase control chart is presented to predict the RUL of mechanical components stage by stage. Experiments on two datasets demonstrate that the proposed method achieves superior RUL prediction performance compared with recently published approaches.
