AB  - Artificial intelligence (AI) is changing how reinforced concrete structures in nuclear facilities are inspected and monitored, which makes AI an additional capability for maintaining safe operation of existing nuclear facilities and small modular reactors (SMRs). Traditional inspection methods rely heavily on manual work and can be error prone. AI technologies, such as machine learning and deep learning, can be configured to detect and quantify damage such as cracks, corrosion, and internal concrete damage by incorporating techniques such as convolutional neural networks (CNNs), faster regionbased CNN, You Only Look Once (YOLO), Attention-UNet, and generative adversarial networks (GAN). These techniques analyze images and data from non-destructive testing (NDT) methods, such as digital cameras, thermography, ground penetrating radar (GPR), ultrasound, and other NDT methods providing a clearer picture of both surface and subsurface damage. However, while these techniques show great potential, significant work is still needed to make them practical in real-world applications. The current drawback of these technologies is the requirement of a large amount of data to train models effectively, which can be difficult to obtain. Challenges related to data volume, data labeling, data variety, the speed at which the data can be acquired, processed, or stored, and ensuring reliable AI models must be addressed. AI can also aid in creating predictive maintenance strategies that can improve efficiency and extend the life of existing structures and future builds. This review concludes by suggesting how AI can enhance inspection processes and the steps needed to apply these technologies effectively in real-world scenarios.
