AB  - The rapid advancement of sensor technologies has made multi-sensory signals easily accessible in various manufacturing systems. To improve health condition assessment and prediction, a variety of neural network-based data fusion approaches have been proposed to generate health indices by fusing multi-sensory signals. However, most of the existing methods focus solely on using health indices to predict system failure times, overlooking their potential application in corresponding operational decision-making. Our focus is on this new usage of health indices to alert users to the warning stage of each system, thereby minimizing decision-making (maintenance) costs. To construct this novel type of health indices, this study proposes a systematic approach based on the concept of a 'low-loss curve' on the loss surface, enabling the training of a fusion neural network that provides health indices yielding both high remaining useful lifetime prediction accuracy and low maintenance costs. The effectiveness of the proposed method is demonstrated using a case study on aircraft turbofan engines, where it successfully reduces maintenance costs without sacrificing prediction accuracy. The code for the proposed methodology will be made publicly available on GitHub upon the publication of this paper. Note to Practitioners-Predicting equipment degradation and making cost-effective maintenance decisions are critical challenges in industrial systems. Existing health index approaches primarily focus on predicting the remaining useful lifetime of a system but often fail to integrate operational decision-making, such as maintenance scheduling and cost optimization. This study introduces a new type of health index, called the decision-oriented operational health index (OHI), designed to simultaneously enhance remaining useful lifetime predictions and minimize maintenance costs. The proposed approach utilizes a neural network-based fusion model that integrates multi-sensor data to construct an OHI that not only predicts system failures but also alerts users when the system enters a warning state, facilitating timely and cost-effective maintenance actions. A key innovation is the introduction of a "low-loss curve" constrained training method, which allows practitioners to select a model that achieves both prediction accuracy and decision-making efficiency. This method offers flexibility, as it can be implemented with various neural network architectures and standard optimization techniques. This methodology can be applied across various industries where predictive maintenance is essential, improving both reliability and cost efficiency in asset management.
