TI  - Artificial Intelligence Empowering Astronomical Observations: Review of Intelligent Telescope Control Systems (Invited)
T2  - LASER & OPTOELECTRONICS PROGRESS
LA  - Chinese
KW  - machine learning
KW  - telescope control
KW  - autonomous observation
KW  - real-time processing
KW  - predictive maintenance
KW  - artificial intelligence
KW  - IMPLEMENTATION
KW  - REINFORCEMENT
AB  - Significance Contemporary astronomy is presently experiencing a profound transformation propelled by the dual forces of next-generation survey technologies and the pressing scientific requirements of time-domain astronomy. Novel observational facilities, including the Zwicky Transient Facility, the Vera C. Rubin Observatory, and the Square Kilometre Array, are ushering the field into an era of big data. The volume of data generated is demonstrating an unprecedented exponential growth pattern; for example, forthcoming large-scale surveys are anticipated to generate petabytes of data throughout their operational durations. Simultaneously, the investigation of transient sources, such as fast radio bursts, gravitational wave counterparts, and tidal disruption events, necessitates the capture of celestial phenomena with extreme transient properties. The scientific significance of these events is exponentially associated with the observation response time, demanding immediate reaction capabilities. In this context, the intrinsic limitations of traditional telescope control systems are becoming increasingly conspicuous. Conventional scheduling modes are predominantly static, depending on offline planning by researchers, which lacks the flexibility to adapt dynamically to real-time weather variations, equipment status fluctuations, and changing scientific priorities. Moreover, the traditional separation of observation and data processing leads to substantial latency, impeding the effective establishment of closed-loop control and resulting in the loss of crucial short-timescale astronomical opportunities. Consequently, the construction of intelligent telescope control systems driven by artificial intelligence has emerged as a crucial direction for surmounting current bottlenecks and promoting the high-quality development of the discipline. By harnessing the superior performance of artificial intelligence in pattern recognition, predictive modeling, and strategy optimization, it is feasible to transform telescopes from static data collectors into autonomous systems capable of real-time perception, decision-making, and self-optimization. Progress Recent technological advancements have witnessed the profound integration of machine learning, deep learning, and reinforcement learning into telescope control systems, propelling substantial progress in three pivotal domains: autonomous observational scheduling, high-precision pointing and tracking, and predictive maintenance. In the realm of autonomous observational scheduling and dynamic optimization, there is a paradigm shift from manual, sequential planning to parallel, closed-loop intelligent scheduling. Machine learning methodologies are extensively employed to mine historical data for the accurate prediction of environmental conditions, such as seeing and cloud cover, thereby providing a quantitative foundation for window selection. More significantly, reinforcement learning has been introduced to tackle complex dynamic scenarios. By establishing an agent-environment-feedback mechanism, systems can autonomously acquire optimal strategies through continuous interaction. This approach is especially effective in responding to sudden transient events, enabling the dynamic adjustment of observation plans within seconds rather than hours. The integration of multi-objective optimization algorithms further empowers the system to balance scientific value, resource consumption, and task complexity, substantially enhancing observational efficiency.
   Concerning telescope pointing and tracking control, artificial intelligence has overcome the limitations of traditional analytical geometry models and classic control theories in dealing with non-linear errors and environmental disturbances. A hybrid architecture that combines physical models with artificial intelligence residual compensation has emerged as the mainstream solution. While traditional models eliminate primary geometric errors, neural networks, specifically those optimized by genetic algorithms or particle swarm optimization, are utilized to fit and compensate for complex non-linear residuals caused by thermal deformation and mechanical hysteresis. For tracking control, particularly in low-speed regimes, radial basis function neural networks have been successfully applied to estimate and compensate for non-linear Stribeck friction in real-time, eliminating velocity jumps at zero-crossing points. Moreover, deep reinforcement learning algorithms, such as deep deterministic policy gradients, have proven effective in suppressing wind load disturbances for large-aperture telescopes by learning optimal control force strategies in variable wind fields. In visual guiding and wavefront sensing, convolutional neural networks have achieved sub-pixel centroid extraction precision even under low signal-to-noise ratio conditions and enabled image-based wavefront sensing without complex hardware sensors. In the field of predictive maintenance, the emphasis has shifted from passive repairs to proactive health management. Machine learning models, including support vector machines and random forests, are utilized for fault prediction through the analysis of multi-dimensional status data. Deep learning models, such as long short-term memory networks, are applied to predict the remaining useful life of critical components. These technologies facilitate the optimization of maintenance strategies and supply chain management, minimizing unplanned downtime and ensuring the continuous and stable operation of observational facilities. Conclusions and Prospects Although the application of artificial intelligence in telescope control has achieved remarkable outcomes, the field encounters challenges related to interpretability, robustness, system integration, and data security. The "black-box" characteristic of numerous deep-learning models presents a hurdle for scientific validation. Therefore, it is imperative to develop interpretable artificial intelligence techniques to establish trust in autonomous decisions, particularly when systems exclude high-priority targets based on internal logic. Robustness is of crucial significance; models must be capable of dealing with out-of-distribution data resulting from sensor malfunctions or extreme weather conditions without experiencing catastrophic failures. This requires the implementation of fail-safe mechanisms that can revert to traditional control methods upon the detection of anomalies. Moreover, the integration of modern artificial intelligence frameworks with legacy control systems necessitates the resolution of compatibility and real-time processing issues. Data security also demands attention to prevent adversarial attacks that may mislead telescope pointing or generate false transient alerts. Looking ahead, the development of intelligent telescope control systems will tend towards greater autonomy, improved real-time processing, and multi-modal fusion. Future systems will attain fully autonomous observation, managing the entire workflow from planning to data analysis without human intervention.
   The integration of edge computing will enable terminal devices to execute high-frequency tasks such as tip-tilt correction and active optics control locally, eliminating network latency. High-performance computing clusters will support the online iteration of scheduling models, realizing a true "optimization based on results" closed-loop system. Furthermore, the in-depth fusion of multi-modal data, which combines optical, radio, and infrared observations with meteorological and geophysical data, will overcome single-source limitations, enhancing decision-making accuracy. Ultimately, human-machine collaboration will evolve, with artificial intelligence handling data-intensive tasks and humans concentrating on complex scientific judgments, supported by interactive interfaces that allow for bidirectional feedback. These advancements will jointly usher modern astronomy into a new era of intelligent, efficient, and autonomous observation.
