AB  - Medical imaging equipment is an essential tool in modern diagnosis, but its reliability and operational safety remain critical concerns to healthcare facilities. Conventional periodic maintenance often fails to predict sudden anomalies, leading to unplanned downtime, compromised image quality, and increased radiation-related risks. While artificial intelligence (AI) has shown promise in clinical diagnostics, its role in predictive maintenance (PdM) of imaging systems remains underexplored. This study proposes a PdM framework called Gated Fusion Network (GFN) for Computed Tomography (CT) equipment using real-time sensor data from Internet of Medical Things (IoMT). The framework facilitates detection and prediction of two critical X-ray tube anomalies: overheating and arcing. It integrates a convolutional neural network (CNN)-based denoising module, gated fusion blocks combining bidirectional long short-term memory (BiLSTM) and Transformer encoders, and a classification head. A large-scale dataset comprising over two million exposures is collected from the CT equipment at West China Hospital between 2019 and 2023. The GFN achieves an F1-score of 0.958 for predicting overheating within a 20-minute window, and an F1-score of 0.632 for arcing prediction approximately up to 1 to 2 days in advance. These findings highlight the potential of AI-enabled IoMT frameworks to reduce operational risk and enhance reliability of critical medical equipment.
