AB  - Predicting indoor CO2 concentrations in education facilities is critical for student health but remains challenging due to complex spatial interactions in CO2 dispersion, especially in spaces with dynamic layout partition configurations for various education activities, which disrupt air flows patterns. Existing CO2 prediction methods often fail to capture the spatial dynamics or adapt to layout changes. To this end, a novel context-aware framework integrating graph-based deep learning and Building Information Modelling (BIM) is proposed for accurate CO2 prediction in reconfigurable education spaces. The approach automatically models both the CO2 sensor network and space configuration contexts by formulating a Voronoi-based CO2 sensor area adjacency graph and constructing a BIM-derived time-varying room layout configuration representation, respectively. Subsequently, a Multi-Modal Deep Learning (MMDL) model is developed to synthesis these multi-source contexts and accurately forecast CO2 concentrations, which are then mapped to air quality guidelines and visualized in the BIM environment for intuitive CO2 condition management. Validated in a reconfigurable university studio with dynamic partitioning, the proposed method outperforms the closest baseline by 25% in Mean Absolute Error (MAE) in the 2-hour prediction horizon. Ablation studies reveal that incorporating the BIM-derived space configuration context improves 2-hour prediction accuracy by 33% in MAE, while the Voronoi-based graph modelling yields a 19% improvement over sensor coordinate-only spatial inputs. Furthermore, the framework demonstrates robust performance across different sensor network densities. This study advances indoor air quality management research by providing a robust and adaptable solution to CO2 concentration prediction in education spaces with dynamic layouts, supporting adaptive and proactive facility management for healthier learning environments.
