AB  - The Digital Twin (DT) concept is emerging as a powerful tool in building automation and management, enabling real-time monitoring, predictive maintenance, and system optimisation to improve overall efficiency, particularly energy efficiency. This paper presents a case study on the application of a DT model for Domestic Hot Water (DHW) control in a small-scale installation equipped with an electric heater. The study investigates the integration of DT with data-driven control strategies, particularly Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) neural networks, to enhance system performance beyond traditional PID control. A test installation was developed, collecting realtime operational data to build and validate the DT model. Various optimisation techniques, including Grey Wolf Optimisation (GWO), Genetic Algorithms (GA), and Particle Swarm Optimisation (PSO)-with a comparative analysis of their performance, were employed to fine-tune the hyperparameters of the LSTM-based predictive model. The results demonstrated that the DT-based control approach reduces fluctuations in water temperature and Control Varaibles, leading to improve predictability, and energy efficiency and user comfort compared to conventional PID control. A key finding of this study is that the DT model, trained on real-world operational data, enables rapid prototyping of new control strategies with minimal risk and lower testing costs, as well as temperature fluctuations reduction by 10.6%. The research also highlights the potential for future applications, including Reinforcement Learning (RL)-based control, where DT serves as an interactive environment for training autonomous control agents.
