AB  - The advancements of artificial intelligence and embedded platforms have enabled the development of cutting-edge solutions for predictive maintenance in the aerospace sector. Nowadays, failure data for the mechanical components of aircraft engines are rather limited. Specifically, the information preceding failures is lacking, as it is difficult and expensive to acquire. To overcome this difficulty and facilitate the development of methodologies based on data-driven approaches for detecting temporal patterns, tools have been developed to simulate the temporal evolution of these components until their failure. This paper presents a new methodology to enhance the throughput inference of prediction models for estimating the Remaining Useful Life of jet engines using a set of simulated data. The proposed models match or outperform most comparison algorithms, demonstrating significant results despite their simpler design and implementation. Subsequently, the models are quantized and compiled for execution on a Deep-learning Processing Unit. While these optimization and deployment steps introduce a slight loss in accuracy, they enable low energy consumption by leveraging the efficiency of the hardware platform. These improvements are crucial for critical embedded systems, such as those required in aerospace applications.
