AB  - The reliable prediction of State of Health (SoH) for Lithium-ion (Li-ion) batteries is critical for advancing sustainable energy technologies, including electric vehicles and grid-scale storage. This paper presents a data-driven framework for battery prognostics that emphasizes model interpretability. Using the NASA battery dataset, a comprehensive feature engineering process was conducted to extract physically meaningful health indicators from raw charge and discharge cycle data. Random Forest (RF) and Gradient Boosting (GB) models were subsequently developed to predict battery degradation. The core of this work lies in the application of Explainable AI (XAI) using SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) to dissect and validate the models' internal logic. The SHAP analysis revealed that features such as average charge current, temperature change during operation, and energy efficiency were the most influential predictors, and their impact on predictions aligns with established electrochemical principles of battery aging. Furthermore, a comparative analysis of the SHAP results demonstrated that while both models achieved high predictive accuracy, they employed distinct reasoning strategies: the RF model used a "democratic" approach, weighing evidence from multiple features, whereas the GB model was more "hierarchical," focusing on the single most dominant indicators. This study underscores the value of XAI in not only building trust in prognostic models but also in providing deeper insights into how different algorithms learn complex degradation phenomena.
