TI  - Chillers fault identification using volunteered minority oversampling via OC-SVM with bounding and minimizing loss
T2  - EXPERT SYSTEMS WITH APPLICATIONS
LA  - English
KW  - Support vector machine loss
KW  - Class balancing
KW  - Predictive maintenance
KW  - False negative rate
KW  - HVAC System
KW  - SUPPORT VECTOR MACHINE
KW  - DIAGNOSIS
KW  - SMOTE
KW  - CHALLENGES
KW  - IMBALANCE
AB  - Class imbalance poses a major challenge in fault detection for Heating, Ventilation, and Air Conditioning (HVAC) systems, as limited fault data skews models and hampers the recognition of minority fault types. To address this, we introduce a Volunteered Minority Sample Generation (VMSG) method that produces balanced datasets for reliable fault classification. VMSG uses One-Class Support Vector Machine (OC-SVM) to learn the system's normal behavior from abundant fault-free data. By measuring distances from the OC-SVM hyperplane, VMSG identifies minority fault instances that best characterize specific fault conditions as "volunteers". These volunteers then help generate synthetic samples to enrich the training set and address class imbalance. Typically, conventional OC-SVM models are sensitive to outliers and computationally expensive due to numerous support vectors. We thus introduce a Bounding and Minimizing (BaM) loss function, which stabilizes the decision boundary, reduces support vectors, and improves efficiency. This OC-SVM integrated VMSG pipeline generates more interpretable samples. An experimental evaluation of the ASHRAE RP1043/1312 datasets shows that the BaM loss outperforms both hinge and rescaled-hinge losses regarding computational efficiency. Additionally, VMSG outperforms popular minority oversampling techniques, including the Synthetic Minority Oversampling Technique (SMOTE), its variants, as well as Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) and Variational Autoencoders (VAEs) for fault classification.
