AB  - Industrial systems require effective anomaly detection and predictive maintenance strategies to prevent unexpected failures and optimize operational efficiency. However, traditional approaches often treat anomaly detection and failure prediction as independent tasks, limiting their ability to leverage operational patterns for early fault diagnosis. This article proposes a novel two-stage hybrid deep learning framework that integrates unsupervised anomaly detection with supervised failure prediction through cross-domain transfer learning. The framework first employs a variational autoencoder (VAE) to extract latent anomaly embeddings from HVAC system operational data without requiring labeled anomalies. These embeddings are transferred and combined with sensor readings from a predictive maintenance dataset to enrich the failure prediction model. Experimental evaluations on two publicly available datasets demonstrate that the proposed hybrid model achieves superior performance compared to conventional methods, with notable improvements in accuracy (90.2%), precision (0.89), recall (0.90), F1-score (0.90), and AUC-ROC (0.873). Additionally, the approach exhibits strong cross-domain generalization ability, highlighting its potential applicability across different industrial environments. This work introduces a scalable and flexible anomaly-driven transfer learning strategy that addresses the scarcity of labeled failure data and enhances the robustness of predictive maintenance systems. The findings suggest that integrating unsupervised operational insights into supervised learning frameworks is a promising direction for advancing intelligent industrial monitoring.
