AB  - Digital twin (DT) technology is emerging as a crucial tool for intelligent fault diagnosis (IFD) and remaining useful life (RUL) prediction in rotating machinery, providing unprecedented support for the reliability and operational efficiency of industrial systems. In industries such as energy, manufacturing, and aerospace, DT enables real-time monitoring, accurate fault diagnosis, and predictive maintenance by creating a virtual counterpart of physical assets. This paper summarizes the latest applications of DT-inspired IFD and RUL prediction, including virtual mathematical model construction, model parameter update, and deep learning technology to connect virtual data and real data for IFD and RUL prediction. Additionally, this paper identifies and discusses key challenges in current DT applications, such as building high-fidelity models, meeting real-time diagnostic demands, and integrating physical and data-driven models. Looking ahead, the advancement of DT models will rely on the integration of multimodal data fusion techniques, the development of lightweight model optimization strategies, and the application of reinforcement learning for autonomous learning and enhanced interpretability in IFD and RUL prediction. This paper provides critical insights to guide further implementation of DT in industrial applications.
