TI  - A data-driven based framework for resilience assessment of hospital HVAC systems
T2  - JOURNAL OF BUILDING ENGINEERING
LA  - English
KW  - Resilience assessment
KW  - Predictive maintenance
KW  - Thermal comfort
KW  - HVAC system
KW  - PERFORMANCE
KW  - ENHANCE
KW  - MODEL
AB  - Hospital HVAC systems are critical to patient comfort, safety, and infection control, yet are frequently challenged by aging infrastructure and inherently complex operating modes. This research describes a new approach to improving hospital HVAC system resilience by incorporating a Psychrometric Comfort Performance Index into a hybrid predictive model based upon Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) networks. Utilizing actual operating data from a functional hospital, the method showcases performance and usability with tangible data. The LSTM model exhibited remarkably accurate predictive performance with correlation values from 0.832 to 0.881 and error values from 0.051 to 0.075, accurately simulating dynamic environment fluctuations affecting HVAC performance. Through integration of psychrometric analysis with deep learning, this solution facilitates effective proactive maintenance strategies to significantly improve system robustness. The work demonstrates how patient safety and operating efficiency can be improved in critical care healthcare settings, allowing facility managers to predict issues in advance and respond effectively to ensure optimal internal conditions. This strategy not only fulfills hospital HVAC system short-term requirements but also establishes foundations for future innovation in healthcare facility operations, allowing HVAC systems to respond to dynamic conditions while maintaining effective patient care.
