TI  - Optimized methods for basic probability assignments in evidence theory: Applications to fault diagnosis
T2  - ENGINEERING APPLICATIONS OF ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE
LA  - English
KW  - Fault diagnosis
KW  - Uncertainty classification
KW  - Dempster-shafer evidence theory
KW  - Basic probability assignment
KW  - Artificial intelligence in real-time systems
KW  - Resource-constrained environments
KW  - CLASSIFICATION
AB  - Evidence theory provides a robust framework for addressing classification problems under high uncertainty, with the generation of basic probability assignments (BPA) being a critical and challenging task. This study introduces novel methods for BPA generation, including a modified non-parametric (MNP) method, a sample-based direct (SD) method, and the modified sample-based direct (MSD) method. The MNP method introduces three strategies - plausibility function generation, integral optimization, and attribute discounting - to enhance BPA generation within the original framework. The SD method eliminates the necessity of estimating attribute probability density functions by directly constructing BPAs from individual samples and integrating them through Dempster's combination rule, thereby enabling asynchronous processing of sample data. Additionally, the MSD method incorporates sample discounting and attribute discounting for further optimization. Experimental evaluations conducted across multiple datasets from the University of California, Irvine Machine Learning Repository (UCI datasets) and a motor rotor fault diagnosis case study demonstrate that the proposed methodologies significantly enhance BPA generation, thereby improving classification accuracy. Furthermore, dynamic sample update experiments reveal that the SD method effectively facilitates sequential processing frameworks, enabling adaptive data adjustments with reduced computational and storage costs. In addition, we performed computational time analysis and parameter sensitivity analysis, providing a comprehensive guideline for method selection. These findings highlight the potential of artificial intelligence to address the needs of various intelligent-driven scenarios, such as real-time systems and resource-constrained environments, predictive maintenance in smart manufacturing, and condition monitoring of industrial machinery.
