AB  - The adoption of artificial intelligence (AI) in facilities management (FM) offers opportunities to enhance operational efficiency, predictive maintenance and resource optimization. However, adoption remains constrained by fragmented systems, unstructured data, low digital maturity and persistent skills gaps. Using an ex ante analytical perspective, this study prioritizes the critical success factors (CSFs) required for effective AI adoption in FM contexts characterized by uneven technological maturity. A first-order literature review identified an initial set of CSFs, which was validated and consolidated through a second-order systematic review (SOSR), resulting in fourteen CSFs. These factors were prioritized using the rough step-wise weight assessment ratio analysis (R-SWARA) method, based on expert judgments from fifteen Brazilian FM professionals. The conceptual model is structured under the technology-organization-environment-human (TOEH) framework. Results show that organizational culture, data availability, strategic alignment and workforce training are the most critical CSFs. The R-SWARA method proved effective in aggregating expert judgments under low-consensus conditions, producing robust prioritization outcomes. The study contributes theoretically by integrating SOSR, TOEH and R-SWARA and practically by supporting decision-making in emerging AI adoption environments.
