AB  - The review article thoroughly examines predictive maintenance models where the conditional probabilities of inspection outcomes rely on prognostics. The demand for effective predictive maintenance models has increased due to various industries adopting data-driven strategies for improved operational effectiveness. The study retraces the historical development of these models up to significant improvements in methodologies to cover uncertainties related to inspections performed in predictive maintenance. Building on previous work, we further analyze how to input conditional probabilities of inspection outcomes into predictive maintenance modeling for better prediction of equipment breakdown and optimization of maintenance scheduling. Comparative analysis highlights a clear trade-off between model accuracy and computational complexity, showing that simpler models with the constant-probability of inspection outcomes are more interpretable but less precise, while advanced models that incorporate inspection timing, dependence on failure time, multiple time-correlated inspections, or machine learning offer higher accuracy at the expense of complexity. This synthesis also identifies the relative advantages and disadvantages of different approaches, proposing guidance for practical application in industrial systems. The article concludes by discussing future research opportunities and the potential of new technologies to enhance predictive maintenance strategies related to imperfect inspections.
