TI  - Remaining useful life prediction using sequential metaheuristic optimization of stacked-LSTM hyperparameters
T2  - CHEMICAL ENGINEERING RESEARCH & DESIGN
LA  - English
KW  - Predictive maintenance
KW  - Long-short term memory
KW  - Sequential optimization
KW  - Metaheuristic algorithm
KW  - Multivariate time series data
KW  - MAINTENANCE FRAMEWORK
KW  - INDUSTRY 4.0
KW  - SYSTEMS
AB  - This study introduces a data-driven predictive maintenance (PdM) method that enhances operational efficiency and extends machine lifespan by optimizing Stacked-Long-Short Term Memory (S-LSTM) hyperparameters sequentially. The technique employs a dual-phase optimization strategy integrating Teaching-Learning-Based Optimization (TLBO) with Particle Swarm Optimization (PSO). The optimization process begins with TLBO, which efficiently navigates the hyperparameter space to identify promising regions, thus laying a solid foundation for the subsequent phase. Then, the process transitions to the PSO phase, where TLBO's outputs serve as the initial parameters for PSO's further optimization. The PSO phase exploits these promising regions identified by TLBO, refining the solutions through a collaborative and competitive process, enabling the S-LSTM model to avoid local optima and converge more reliably towards near optimum. This approach enhances prediction accuracy, reliability, and scalability across various dataset sizes and complexities, facilitating the early detection and prediction of future failures. It enables timely preventive actions and optimizing maintenance strategies. The proposed method's performance is demonstrated using the NASA C-MAPSS dataset, achieving an accuracy of 98.9% and precision up to 100% in binary and multi-class classifications, thereby outperforming existing models.
