AB  - This study investigates recent advancements at the intersection of digital twins and machine learning to enhance the performance, reliability, and scalability of renewable hydrogen systems. This document elucidates how digital twins (DTs) create real-time virtual replicas of assets such as wind turbines, solar panels, electrolysers, storage systems, and hydrogen networks through machine-learning (ML) models to facilitate predictions, anomaly detection, degradation monitoring, and operational decision-making. Emerging developments include real-time twins for electrolysers, hybrid physics-data modelling frameworks, and sophisticated analytics for production planning and safety. The lack of standardized validation benchmarks, interoperability challenges, model drift, cyber-physical issues, security risks, and insufficient data availability and sharing have been significant impediments for hydrogen generation through renewable energy sources. A structured roadmap is proposed to guide the future development of DT-ML ecosystems, emphasizing architectural design, uncertaintyaware modelling, and policy frameworks that facilitate the safe and extensive deployment of hydrogen.
