TI  - Development of Artificial Intelligence to Machine Vibration Analysis via The Internet of Things System
T2  - PROCEEDINGS OF THE 2025 12TH INTERNATIONAL CONFERENCE ON MECHANICS, MATERIALS AND MANUFACTURING, ICMMM2025
LA  - English
CP  - 12th International Conference on Mechanics, Materials and Manufacturing-ICMMM
KW  - Artificial Intelligence
KW  - Vibration
KW  - Internet of Things
KW  - Predictive Maintenance
KW  - SUPPORT
AB  - Artificial Intelligence (AI) has become a crucial technology in the development and optimization of various systems, particularly in the detection and analysis of machine anomalies. AI enables the analysis and prediction of potential issues with high accuracy, allowing for the prevention of damage and the reduction of maintenance costs. By leveraging AI to detect vibrations in machinery, inspections and analyses can be conducted swiftly and accurately. Basic machinery used in industrial plants, such as pumps and motors, typically operate through rotational motion. When malfunctions occur, they often manifest as abnormal vibrations. However, detecting these vibrations is challenging, as such machines usually lack sensors to monitor vibrations, and these devices tend to be expensive. In this research, AI is applied to analyze vibration data from machinery, with the Internet of Things (IoT) playing a supporting role in connecting sensors and transmitting data through the internet for continuous monitoring with interval and analysis. The objective of this study is to develop a system that utilizes AI for vibration analysis, using ADXL345 Accelerometers and ESP32 microcontrollers, which offer both high performance and cost-effectiveness. The vibration data collected from an experimental motor setup is processed into the frequency domain to facilitate the analysis and detection of anomalies in the machinery system. The dataset is then divided, and AI models are applied for use on both computers and microcontrollers. A web-based reporting system was created to integrate the devices through a network. In real-world scenarios, data collection is typically limited to normal operating conditions. Therefore, a model trained on normal operation data aligns well with practical applications. Experimental results revealed that the best-performing model on a computer was a hybrid model combining One-Class SVM with SVM, achieving a Recall (anomaly detection rate) of 100.00% in the first step and an average accuracy of 98.65% for classifying normal conditions in the second step. For microcontroller applications, the best model was One-Class SVM, which was optimized to fit within the limited memory of the microcontroller. It achieved a Recall of 92.50% for detecting abnormal conditions. This study demonstrates the feasibility of developing a cost-effective vibration monitoring system using IoT technology and AI models. The system can be implemented on computers and embedded microcontrollers, enhancing maintenance strategies in industrial applications and driving smart systems.
