TI  - ReNo: Optimal regularized normalization with endogenous distribution matching under low-quality data for remaining useful life prognostics
T2  - MECHANICAL SYSTEMS AND SIGNAL PROCESSING
LA  - English
KW  - Low-quality data
KW  - Normalization
KW  - AI for industry
KW  - Remaining useful life prediction
KW  - MODEL
AB  - Predictive maintenance and reliability analytics are emerging as key paradigms for sustaining complex engineered systems, with remaining useful life (RUL) prediction as a core task for health management and intelligent decision-making. In practical industrial scenarios, time series signals are often affected by noise and exhibit low-quality characteristics. As a widely adopted preprocessing technique, normalization aims to eliminate dimensional differences and improve the comparability of data in the input space. However, traditional methods such as min-max and z-score normalization rely on fixed statistics and often fail to maintain scale consistency under noise-dominated conditions. In this paper, we propose ReNo, an Optimal Regularized Normalization with Endogenous Distribution Matching. We first analyze the failure modes of conventional normalization under low-quality data and unify them within a latent optimization formulation. Building on this insight, we endogenously construct the discrepancy between the observed distribution and a reference distribution as a KL-divergence-based regularization term, thereby suppressing scale estimation bias without resorting to explicit noise separation. Additionally, we introduce a general RUL prediction framework and design a simple yet effective Degradation-Aware Representation Amplifier (DARA) as an instantiation of the time series encoder, achieving state-of-the-art performance on multiple public datasets. Extensive experiments further demonstrate that ReNo yields consistent improvements across various backbone models, validating its effectiveness as a general and robust normalization method for data-driven predictive maintenance in complex industrial systems.
