TI  - Development of the Dynamic Analogous Reference System (DARS) for thermoeconomic diagnosis in buildings thermal systems
T2  - JOURNAL OF BUILDING ENGINEERING
LA  - English
KW  - Analogous reference system
KW  - Artificial intelligence
KW  - Machine learning
KW  - Thermoeconomic diagnosis
KW  - OPERATION
AB  - This study develops an accessible and low-cost computational dynamic model of building thermal systems using machine learning. The main objective is to create a Dynamic Analogous Reference System (DARS) to compare it with the current operation system, detect anomalies and quantify its effects in real time. DARS considers the flexible behaviour of thermal systems in buildings and models the components using machine learning to predict thermodynamic variables in real time, adapting to operational conditions, including extreme scenarios and functioning with a limited number of sensors. The code is applied to a case study of a facility supplying DHW demand, where multilayer perceptron models are trained for components such as the boiler, heat exchanger, and storage tank. For the multilayer perceptron models, very low mean squared errors are achieved: 1.11e-3 for the boiler, 1.25e-9 for the heat exchanger and 2.93e-4 for the tank under normal conditions. Extreme conditions are included to validate the tool's adaptability, where, although unlikely, the DHW demand is doubled so the model's predictions are compared with TRNSYS simulations. The results show strong performance, with normalised prediction errors below 0.05 compared to those obtained through simulation. It is concluded that integrating machine learning enables the model to evolve with historical and real-time data, without major investments in infrastructure. Additionally, the proposed approach facilitates predictive maintenance through thermoeconomic diagnosis, offering a valuable tool for energy optimisation in buildings with limited resources. This work contributes to the democratisation of energy efficiency technologies, supporting global sustainability goals and climate change mitigation.
