TI  - Improving the performance of green office buildings in mainland China through facility management under the "dual carbon" goals
T2  - JOURNAL OF CLEANER PRODUCTION
LA  - English
KW  - Facility management
KW  - Carbon reduction capability
KW  - Green office buildings
KW  - Digital twin
KW  - Policy
KW  - China
KW  - EMISSIONS
AB  - This study examines how facility management (FM), influenced by national policy and technological advancements, can enhance the carbon reduction capacity (CRC) of green office buildings in mainland China. Addressing a significant research gap, it examines the operational phase of green buildings, often neglected in traditional design-focused evaluations. A novel theoretical framework, Integrated Technology Acceptance and Policy Change Theory (I-TAM-PCT), is introduced to analyze the institutional and technological drivers of FM effectiveness. A hybrid analytical method, Entropy-TOPSIS-Machine Learning (ET-ML), is applied to 203 survey responses and nine expert interviews. A composite index, the Integrated Carbon Reduction Performance Index (ICRPI), was developed using 16 dimensions from policy and technology indicators. Among eight evaluated algorithms, Gradient Boosting achieved the highest accuracy (0.89), with SHAP analysis indicating that policy incentives, responsiveness, and Digital Twin-based FM (DTFM) features, especially real-time consumption monitoring and predictive optimization, are the most significant factors in enhancing CRC. Notably, buildings implementing DTFM showed an average CRC improvement of 18.4 % compared to non-adopters. By integrating a multi-level policy-technology framework with a reliable computational model, this study advances both theoretical understanding and practical approaches for carbon-neutral building operations. The findings offer valuable insights for policymakers and practitioners in China and other developing economies pursuing low-carbon growth through intelligent FM.
