TI  - Uncertainty-aware multi-modal axial fan fault diagnosis: fusion of acoustic fingerprinting with transformer-BiGRU-Bayesian architecture
T2  - MEASUREMENT
LA  - English
KW  - Axial fan
KW  - Fault diagnosis
KW  - Multi-modal fusion
KW  - Acoustic fingerprinting
KW  - Transformer-BiGRU-Bayesian
AB  - To address the limitations of traditional single-modal diagnostic techniques and the difficulty of uncertainty quantification in deterministic models for axial fan fault diagnosis in thermal power plants, a multi-modal fault diagnosis framework integrating acoustic fingerprinting technology and a Transformer-BiGRU-Bayesian hybrid architecture is proposed. First, features are extracted from current-voltage and vibration signals, and an acoustic fingerprint model is constructed to capture fault acoustic characteristics through short-time Fourier transform, Mel-frequency cepstral coefficients, and Gaussian mixture modeling for highly discriminative state representation. Second, a multi-modal feature fusion strategy is designed, combining F-score feature selection with nonlinear transformation to achieve deep integration of current-voltage, vibration, and acoustic features. Finally, a Transformer-BiGRU-Bayesian hybrid architecture is constructed, integrating attention mechanisms, bidirectional sequence modeling, and Mahalanobis distance-based Bayesian inference to realize fault identification and uncertainty quantification. The novelty of this work lies in the development of a non-contact acoustic fingerprinting technique for fault monitoring, the design of an effective multi-modal fusion framework that exploits complementary advantages of heterogeneous signals, and the introduction of a hybrid deep probabilistic architecture that simultaneously provides accurate diagnostics and reliable uncertainty estimation. Experiments conducted on an actual thermal power plant axial fan test platform demonstrate that the proposed method achieves a diagnostic accuracy of 98.45%, with improvements of 3.10% to 22.48% compared to TimesNet, BiLSTM-Attention, MSCNN, Transformer-BiGRU, Transformer, BiGRU, GRU, and LSTM, respectively and Ablation studies confirm the effectiveness of each architectural component. In terms of uncertainty estimation, the calibration error is merely 0.23, achieving high consistency between prediction confidence and actual reliability. With increasing noise levels, the proposed model exhibits the smallest decline in diagnostic accuracy, demonstrating excellent anti-interference capability. Meanwhile, the model shows strong generalization capability across different datasets. These results indicate that the multi-modal fusion with hybrid deep probabilistic architecture significantly enhances the accuracy, reliability, and robustness of axial fan fault diagnosis in thermal power plants, providing a novel approach to equipment condition monitoring and predictive maintenance in power generation facilities.
