TI  - Impact of common operational faults on the energy performance of split-type air conditioners in tropical island climates: A simulation study in Madagascar and Reunion Island
T2  - ENERGY REPORTS
LA  - English
KW  - Split-type air conditioners
KW  - HVAC system faults
KW  - Energy overconsumption
KW  - Tropical island climate
KW  - Simulation-based analysis
KW  - EnergyPlus and OpenStudio
KW  - Fault detection and diagnosis (FDD)
KW  - Predictive maintenance strategies
KW  - SYSTEMS
KW  - DIAGNOSIS
AB  - The increasing use of air conditioning systems, especially in tropical regions, raises energy and environmental concerns. The presence of faults in compressor systems, sometimes unnoticed by the user, can significantly increase the energy consumption of an air conditioning system. This study evaluates the energy impact of some common faults in vapor compression systems using simulations conducted with OpenStudio and EnergyPlus software. The simulations were applied to various climatic zones in Madagascar and Reunion Island, covering coastal cities and highland areas. The results show that coastal thermal conditions exacerbate condensation-related faults, while pressure-related faults are amplified by the thermal conditions of the highlands. In critical cases, energy overconsumption reaches 21.1% compared to a fault-free scenario. Faults can result in an annual additional cost equivalent to three months of energy consumption and generate 347 kg of extra CO2 emissions. This study provides relevant indicators that help improve the efficient use of air conditioners and supports the development of predictive maintenance strategies based on a low-cost, easy-to-implement approach.
