TI  - Construction Work Plan Prediction for Facility Management Using Text Mining
T2  - COMPUTING IN CIVIL ENGINEERING 2017: SMART SAFETY, SUSTAINABILITY, AND RESILIENCE
LA  - English
CP  - ASCE International Workshop on Computing in Civil Engineering (IWCCE)
KW  - Text mining
KW  - Facility management
KW  - Work prediction
KW  - Machine learning
KW  - Construction
AB  - Infrastructure planning and management teams need to promptly diagnose and resolve building problems when they occur. A common way to notice building problems is receiving service requests from occupants in terms of phone calls, emails, or specific information systems for facility management. Usually, facility management teams need to manually discern the request's priority level, and decide necessary crews and tasks to solve the problem. However, decision making in response to the service requests is often problematic and delayed due to intensive labor demand. This paper demonstrates an effort to automatically predict the reaction type (regular or urgent) and the necessary crews through mining textual descriptions of service requests. Service request data were collected from a university's facility management team. The dataset contains approximately 80,000 instances across two years. Multiple machine learning algorithms are employed to identify the optimal model to predict work priority and crew types. Findings suggest the model successfully and automatically determine critical patterns extracted from service request texts. The text-mining-based prediction model enables facility management to expedite the construction work process. The model is also scalable to other fields of construction management and facility management.
